{
"dur": 75.77,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 8.76,
"text": "So what protects the people spending the money? Whatnot did add spending limits and watch-time limits. But only in December twenty twenty-five.",
"end": 9.035
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 9.035,
"vo_start": 9.31,
"vo_end": 21.19,
"text": "And there are gaps. Ten dollars under your limit, you can still bid a hundred, and it goes through. Bids are binding, Whatnot says, like any auction. And the limit can be raised right before you hit it.",
"end": 21.465
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 21.465,
"vo_start": 21.74,
"vo_end": 31.1,
"text": "You have to be eighteen to hold an account. But with permission, a thirteen-year-old can use a parent's. And the younger someone meets gambling, the higher the risk of problems later on.",
"end": 31.375
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 31.375,
"vo_start": 31.65,
"vo_end": 41.29,
"text": "Then there's the design. You swipe to bid. A countdown ticks. The chat cheers you on, and the host is hyping the next pack before the last one has even landed.",
"end": 41.565
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 41.565,
"vo_start": 41.84,
"vo_end": 53.32,
"text": "Experts call these dark patterns: design that nudges you into choices you might not make with a clear head. Digital law expert Dr Mark Leiser says the pressure adds up to a degradation of consent.",
"end": 53.595
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 53.595,
"vo_start": 53.87,
"vo_end": 65.71,
"text": "He'd like your running total always on screen, warnings before big bids, and more friction when spending suddenly speeds up. A click, he says, shows what you did. Not whether you had a fair chance to decide.",
"end": 65.985
},
{
"i": 7,
"start": 65.985,
"vo_start": 66.26,
"vo_end": 74.82,
"text": "Whatnot says its account controls let every buyer see their total spend and set limits. But it didn't answer whether it flags rapid, repeated purchases.",
"end": 75.77
}
],
